WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.192
This ERD shows the logical blueprint for the clinic.

00:00:03.192 --> 00:00:06.487
Patient and provider each connect to appointment.

00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:14.252
Appointment then connects to service through the appointment service junction table.

00:00:14.252 --> 00:00:19.912
Read each relationship separately rather than trying to understand the entire diagram at once.

00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:23.037
Patient to appointment is one-to-many.

00:00:23.037 --> 00:00:24.467
Provider to appointment is one-to-many.

00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:30.282
Appointment to service is many-to-many, so appointment service sits between them.

00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:37.647
Notice that appointment service also stores facts about the relationship, such as quantity and unit charge.

00:00:37.647 --> 00:00:42.877
Those values do not belong only to the appointment or only to the service.

00:00:42.877 --> 00:00:47.152
They belong to the specific service used in a specific appointment.

00:00:47.152 --> 00:00:54.007
That is one reason junction tables are often real business entities rather than only technical connectors.

00:00:54.007 --> 00:00:59.337
Ask which side can exist many times for one row on the other side.

00:00:59.337 --> 00:01:03.002
That determines where the foreign key normally belongs.

00:01:03.002 --> 00:01:07.157
In an order system, many order lines belong to one order,

00:01:07.157 --> 00:01:10.832
so the order-line table carries the order identifier.

00:01:10.832 --> 00:01:15.760
The same reasoning applies to encounters, enrollments, and transactions.
